Polymorphism is one of the four main pillars of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP).

Definition: Polymorphism means "one name, many forms." It allows the same method name or interface to represent different behaviors.

The word polymorphism comes from:

Poly = Many

Morph = Forms

Real-World Example

Consider a person who can perform different roles:

At college, the person is a student.

At home, the person is a family member.

At work, the person is an employee.

Similarly, in Java, the same method call can produce different behavior depending on the arguments or the actual object.
